title: Checkmk Monitoring Compliance
agents: linux, windows
catalog: app/checkmk
license: GPLv2
distribution:
description:
 This service shows, per host, which subsystems are installed or running and
 could be monitored by Checkmk, but are not yet monitored.

 Detection is capability-based rather than relying on a static application
 list. Every data source contributes typed capabilities (type + name), which
 are correlated to the check plug-ins available on the site via a normalized
 name token and an alias table. Any program whose name matches an available
 plug-in is therefore detected automatically.

 Detection sources:

 - Existing agent sections that are present on the host.
 - Running systemd service units (agent plug-in or built-in section) and,
   on Windows, running services (via the Windows agent plug-in).
 - Running processes (ps / ps_lnx).
 - Host labels (read server-side via Livestatus).
 - Installed packages from the HW/SW inventory (read server-side by the
   special agent) and from the optional agent plug-ins.

 The check does not bind the cached lnx_packages / win_reg_uninstall agent
 sections, so the service is driven by live data and recomputes on the normal
 check interval (and can be rescheduled). Installed-package data still only
 changes as fast as the HW/SW inventory is refreshed.

 Optional agent plug-ins provide a clean capability section: a shell plug-in
 for Linux/Unix and a PowerShell plug-in for Windows. Verbose names (e.g.
 Windows display names such as "Microsoft SQL Server 2019") are matched to
 plug-in tokens via a signature table.

 A server-side data source (special agent) determines which plug-ins are
 active on the host, which plug-ins the site knows, the host labels, and the
 inventory packages.

 All detected capabilities are written to a persistent Capability Database
 (default $OMD_ROOT/var/monitoring_compliance/capability_db.json). It grows
 continuously, deduplicates entries by type and name, records on which hosts
 each capability was seen, and stores whether it is monitorable / monitored.

 Resulting states (overridable via rule):

 - {WARN}: present, not running and not monitored.
 - {CRIT}: running, but not monitored.

 The metric "compliance_percent" reflects the share of monitored subsystems
 among the monitorable subsystems present on the host. The service details
 (long output) list each monitored subsystem (with its plug-in) and each
 unmonitored subsystem with a hint and a "detected via" breakdown showing the
 capabilities (process, systemd unit, service, package, label) that triggered
 it, so the ignore rules can be configured precisely.

item:
 No item. There is exactly one service per host.

discovery:
 One service "Checkmk Monitoring Compliance" is created as soon as the
 server-side data source delivers data.
